optFlagsT -- SEQ 454 R1 (Tony, 2026-10-29): BN-3's certificate, pre-registered on trunk before the cure.

One action per format. pop.sh runs this file twice, one process each: as it is (the old road -- no parser() call, so every
action body is parsed by the interpreted rule walk), and with parser(Start) inserted after the search line (the new road
-- every action body is parsed by the generated parser at its first call, baselineTestsNew's shape). A row compares the
new road's OF-n line with the old road's. The old road is the oracle for PARSING here; what the formats print on either
road is not under test (the old road pads `OF-1 [` too, which is its own business).

  OF-1 #12s   OF-2 #s   OF-3 #.3f   OF-4 #8.2f   OF-5 #5d    no flags   -- born red: the action fails to parse
  OF-6 #-12s  OF-7 #-s  OF-8 #012d                           flags      -- agree today, and must stay

THE DEFECT (SEQ 453, measured): a set term with a repeat after its brackets (`flags?=[-# 0+']+`) is stored as a group
holding the set (aCTionTraiTdata.scannerKeepsDatA: the repeat lives on the held DatA), and setParseWalk's
`case isGROUP: field.parseMethod = null;` installs no method. The term then runs driveStep's old-road arm, whose miss
returns labelNO and never reads the term's `?`, so the && chain fails. A flagged format matches, so it never misses.
THE CURE (SEQ 454 option a): the group gets the method its set would get, and the miss reaches exitFromParse.
